<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/country.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/country.png" alt="" width="840" height="519" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-119448" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/country.png 1000w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/country-300x186.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/country-768x475.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/country-825x510.png 825w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>I finally took a peek at my outstanding issues for eplot on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/eplot" data-cached-time="2026-01-21T19:40:36" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/cache-2026-01-21-web.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">Microsoft Github</a> again, and <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/eplot/issues/16" data-cached-time="2026-01-21T20:11:08" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/cache-2026-01-21-web-2.webp" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">somebody asked</a> the very reasonable question:  Why can&#8217;t eplot just do the right thing, as if by magic, with files like this:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/2026-01-21.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/2026-01-21.jpeg" alt="" width="616" height="347" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-119449" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/2026-01-21.jpeg 616w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/2026-01-21-300x169.jpeg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 616px) 100vw, 616px" /></a></p>
<p>Why not indeed?  So I&#8217;ve tinkered a bit, and things should work pretty automatically now.  That is, load of a CSV file like that, <tt>M-x eplot-mode</tt> and then <tt>C-c C-c</tt>, and viola (enclicken to play video (don&#8217;t worry, no audio)):</p>
<p><video controls loop muted><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/video-6f3Q8U.mp4" poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/posterTsc1fW001.jpg"  type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>The idea being that eplot should have sensible defaults, but then you can tinker with the look by hitting <tt>l</tt> to create controls, then just edit away until you have to look you&#8217;re looking for (apparently I went for a &#8220;bright goth&#8221; thing above), and then export the results.</p>
<p>So there you go.  Hopefully I didn&#8217;t break anything much&#8230;</p>
